<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Crucible</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Metal is not optional here: the simulation is drawn with it and there is no
		     fallback path. Stating it means the App Store will not offer the app to a
		     device that cannot run it. -->
		<string>metal</string>
	</array>
	<!-- An empty launch screen dictionary rather than a storyboard. Without one of the two
	     iOS runs the app in a compatibility mode that letterboxes it, which on a device
	     with rounded corners and an island looks broken. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string></string>
	</dict>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIUserInterfaceStyle</key>
	<string>Dark</string>
	<!-- The simulation fills the screen, so the status bar would sit on top of it. -->
	<key>UIStatusBarHidden</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- Lets the app actually draw at the display's full rate.
	     Without this, iOS silently caps everything at 60 frames a second on a ProMotion
	     iPhone no matter what the app asks for — so a 120Hz screen runs at half speed and
	     the only clue is a frame interval of 16.7ms when the work took under one. -->
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Nothing here switches the Metal performance overlay off. It is driven by iOS's own
	     developer settings and is left entirely under your control. -->
	<!-- The two typefaces the web version loads from Google's servers, bundled instead,
	     because an app has to look right with no network. Both are under the SIL Open Font
	     License; its text sits beside the files in App/Resources/Fonts.

	     A name missing from this list does not fail the build and does not crash: the app
	     just silently draws in the system face instead. LabFonts.missingFaces() checks for
	     that at runtime and the debug readout reports it. -->
	<!-- Required before iOS will hand over any motion reading. Without it the tilt control is
	     refused rather than merely unavailable, and the refusal is silent. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Tilt your phone to change which way things fall, and shake it to rattle a pile loose. If you switch the room's senses on, Crucible also reads the barometer and the day's step count as part of the physics: the air pressure sets the weather in the powder world, and the walking sets how strong the sea's tide is. Nothing is sent anywhere.</string>
	<!-- Asked for only when somebody chooses "Save Image" or "Save Video" on a picture or a clip they are sharing.
	     Without it that choice in the system's share sheet closes the app outright, because iOS treats saving to the
	     photo library with no reason given as a privacy violation. Adding to the library only: nothing is read. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Crucible saves the pictures and movie clips you choose to keep into your photo library. It never looks at the photos already there.</string>
	<!-- Both of the next two keys are required before a shared room will work at all, and iOS gives no
	     error when they are missing: advertising simply never finds anybody and looking never sees
	     anybody. There is nothing to debug because nothing fails.

	     NSBonjourServices must list the service under BOTH tcp and udp. MultipeerConnectivity uses
	     whichever suits the link — wifi or Bluetooth — and declaring only one means it works in some
	     rooms and not others, which is a far worse problem to be handed than it never working at all.

	     The name must match RoomTransport.serviceType exactly. -->
	<!-- The address the sign-in sheet watches for. When OAuth finishes, the server's last redirect
	     goes to crucible://auth?token=… — the system sheet matches this scheme, stops, and hands the
	     address to the app rather than fetching it. That is what carries the session across from a
	     web view whose cookies the app can never see.

	     The word must match CloudSignIn.scheme in the app and nativeCallbackScheme on the server.
	     Getting it wrong shows up as a sign-in sheet that opens, completes, and then just sits
	     there — so check all three when that happens. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.crucible.lab.auth</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>crucible</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!-- A world as a file of its own kind, so one sent in a message or saved in Files opens in Crucible with a tap
	     rather than asking what it is. The contents are the same JSON the app has always written; only the name
	     is its own. "Owner" because nothing else makes these. A copy is taken rather than the file opened where
	     it is, which is simpler and cannot leave the sender's copy half-written. None of this needs an
	     entitlement or anything else that makes installing the app harder. -->
	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.crucible.lab.world</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Crucible world</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.json</string>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>crucible</string>
				</array>
				<key>public.mime-type</key>
				<array>
					<string>application/x-crucible-world</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
			<string>Crucible world</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>LSHandlerRank</key>
			<string>Owner</string>
			<key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.crucible.lab.world</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>LSSupportsOpeningDocumentsInPlace</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Crucible looks for other phones nearby so you can share a world with somebody in the same room. Nothing is sent over the internet.</string>
	<key>NSBonjourServices</key>
	<array>
		<string>_crucible-room._tcp</string>
		<string>_crucible-room._udp</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>Syne-Medium.ttf</string>
		<string>Syne-SemiBold.ttf</string>
		<string>Syne-Bold.ttf</string>
		<string>IBMPlexSans-Regular.ttf</string>
		<string>IBMPlexSans-Medium.ttf</string>
		<string>IBMPlexSans-SemiBold.ttf</string>
		<string>IBMPlexMono-Regular.ttf</string>
		<string>IBMPlexMono-Medium.ttf</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Asked for only when somebody switches the field's music reaction on, and worth being plain
	     about: the sound is never recorded, never saved and never leaves the phone. It is turned into
	     three numbers — how much low end, how much middle, how loud — and those are thrown away again
	     every frame. -->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>With Listen on, the particle field moves to the music the microphone hears. With Talk to it on, the lab listens for what you say to it — "make it rain", "boom" — and does it. Sound is turned into numbers or words on the phone and never kept or sent anywhere.</string>
	<!-- Only asked for when Talk to it is switched on. Recognition happens on the phone itself wherever the phone can
	     do that. -->
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>With Talk to it on, what you say to the lab is turned into words so it can do what you ask. Where the phone can, this happens on the phone itself, and nothing is kept.</string>
	<!-- Only asked for when waving, looking round with your head, or putting the world on a table is switched on. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>The front camera lets you wave at the particle field and look round it by moving your head; the back camera stands the powder world on a real table. Pictures are looked at on the phone as they arrive and are never kept or sent anywhere.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
